## PART 10: JURY VIVA — 150+ QUESTION BANK

### SECTION A: PROBLEM & MOTIVATION (Questions 1–15)

**Q1. Why is this problem important for India?**
India has 1.4 billion people but consistently underperforms at international sports events. A key reason is that talent identification happens too late, relies on expensive professional equipment, is geographically concentrated in cities, and has no standardized data format. SAI identified this through SIH Problem Statement 25073.

**Q2. How does your solution solve the talent identification bottleneck?**
A smartphone can now replicate a professional fitness assessment in a village. The athlete uploads a 30-second video, MediaPipe extracts 33 body landmarks per frame, our CV engine measures the exact physical metric, the scoring engine normalizes it against age/sex peers, and the result is stored in a verified, searchable database accessible to scouts nationwide.

**Q3. What existing solutions exist? How is yours different?**
Existing tools: expensive force plates (₹5–15 lakh), dedicated timing gates, SAI's own assessment camps (accessible to only ~50,000 athletes/year). Our solution: works on any Android/iOS phone, free, offline-capable, fraud-resistant with HMAC certificates, and scalable to millions.

**Q4. What is the "democratization" claim you make?**
It removes three barriers: economic (no equipment cost), geographic (works offline in rural areas), and access (any athlete self-registers and takes tests, not just those selected for SAI camps).

**Q5. How many athletes could this platform realistically reach?**
SAI currently assesses ~50,000–1,00,000 athletes per year through physical camps. A smartphone-based platform with offline capability could realistically reach all 25 crore+ school-going children who participate in Khelo India or state-level programs.

**Q6. What happens if the athlete cheats? How do you detect it?**
See Section 3.3. We have 6 anti-cheat layers: speed spikes (edited video), position discontinuities (cut frames), person consistency (switching athletes mid-video), insufficient frames (too short a video), athlete disappearance (leaving frame), and protocol violation detection. The validation score (0–100%) is printed on every certificate.

**Q7. What is your benchmark source? Are these SAI's official numbers?**
Honest answer: No. They are prototype estimates aligned to published international batteries (AAHPERD, Khelo India, Eurofit, WHO/IAP). This is clearly labelled on every API response and certificate. The engine and interpolation math are production-ready; replacing the numbers requires SAI to share their percentile tables. A step-by-step integration checklist is in `docs/BENCHMARKS.md`.

**Q8. Why MediaPipe and not a custom CNN?**
MediaPipe BlazePose runs in real-time on a phone CPU/GPU, requires zero training data from us, handles diverse body types, and has proven accuracy on human pose estimation (Google's production solution). Building a custom model would require thousands of labeled sports-specific videos and months of training. MediaPipe gives us a production-quality foundation immediately, and our domain-specific logic (FSM, kinematics, etc.) sits on top.

**Q9. What is the "Athletic Performance Index"? How is it computed?**
It is a single 0–100 score summarizing an athlete's overall fitness. It's computed as a weighted geometric mean of normalized scores across 5 domains: speed (sprint), agility (shuttle run), strength (sit-up), power (average of vertical + broad jump), and endurance (600 m run). Geometric mean is used because it penalizes a very weak domain more than an arithmetic mean — an athlete who is excellent at 4 things but terrible at 1 scores lower than an all-rounder.

**Q10. What does "normalized score" mean?**
It's a percentile: a score of 70 means this athlete performed better than 70% of their age and sex peer group (based on reference norms). Raw scores (e.g., "32 sit-ups") are meaningless without context; a 10-year-old doing 32 and a 17-year-old doing 32 are very different performers.

**Q11. Why offline support? Why not just require internet?**
40% of rural India has unreliable internet. If a coach assesses 30 students at a sports camp in Rajasthan with spotty connectivity, they shouldn't lose data. Our PWA caches the app shell and stores submitted videos in IndexedDB. When connectivity returns, videos are automatically submitted to the backend.

**Q12. How does the certificate prevent fraud?**
The assessment details (athlete ID, test type, raw score, normalized score, status, timestamp) are hashed using HMAC-SHA256 with a server-only secret key. The hash is stored in the database and printed on the certificate. The public `/verify/{id}` endpoint recomputes the hash from the current database row and compares it — any tampering (even changing a single digit of the score) produces a mismatch. The QR code on the certificate takes the verifier directly to this endpoint.

**Q13. How do scouts discover talent?**
Scouts (officials) log in with a special account and use the Scout Dashboard. They can filter athletes by state, district, minimum overall performance index, and specific test type. The API returns ranked athlete cards with their scores.

**Q14. What is the SMS OTP system for?**
Password recovery. When an athlete forgets their password, they request an OTP to their registered phone number. The OTP is sent via Twilio SMS (primary) or Fast2SMS (fallback). It expires in 10 minutes and can only be used once. After 5 failed OTP attempts, the athlete is locked out.

**Q15. Who is the target user? Is this just for elite athletes?**
No. The target is primarily school-age athletes (8–18 years) who have never been formally assessed. It works equally for a 9-year-old in a village and an 18-year-old regional champion.

